<reference_puzzle index="1" source="train" puzzle_id="8886d717" lines="43-63">
<description>
Repair markers embedded in the main object and copy the remaining markers one pixel toward the solid edge.

Input structure:
- A mostly background grid contains one solid non-background edge line.
- Apart from that edge and the background, there is one large main object
  color and a smaller marker color.
- Each marker is either embedded in the main object, touching it on at
  least three 4-neighbor sides, or isolated on the background.

Rule:
- Embedded markers are recolored to the main object color.
- Isolated background markers remain in place and also paint the adjacent
  background pixel in the direction of the solid edge line.
- All other pixels are unchanged.

Key insight:
The full edge line is a direction cue: it tells which way the background
markers extend, while markers surrounded by the main object are simply
defects to be filled.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="58c02a16" lines="10-26">
<description>
Expand the top-left seed mask into a recursive colored pattern and tile it across the full grid.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- All foreground lies in a top-left rectangle whose last row and last column are a solid secondary color.
- Removing that secondary-color row and column leaves a seed region containing background plus one primary color.

Rule
- Use the primary-color pixels in the seed as a binary mask.
- Replace each primary seed cell with a copy of the seed mask in the primary color, and each background seed cell
  with a copy of the seed mask in the secondary color.
- Repeat and crop that expanded tile to the original grid shape.

Key insight
The solid L-shaped secondary-color border is a legend, not output content; it supplies the color used when the
seed's background cells are expanded.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="342ae2ed" lines="34-49">
<description>
Draw the missing diagonal connector segment between each pair of same-color rectangular objects.

Input structure:
- The grid has one dominant background color.
- Every foreground color appears as exactly two separated solid rectangular 4-connected objects.
- The two rectangles of each color are diagonally offset with facing corners on a 45-degree line.

Rule:
- Keep all existing rectangles unchanged.
- For each foreground color, connect the lower facing corner of the upper rectangle to the upper facing corner of the lower rectangle.
- Paint only the intervening background pixels on that diagonal, excluding the two endpoint pixels already in the rectangles.

Key insight:
- Same-color rectangles define the endpoints; the output completes the open diagonal between their nearest facing corners.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="eval" puzzle_id="89565ca0" lines="120-136">
<description>
Convert each colored partitioned frame into a horizontal bar whose length is its number of enclosed regions.

Input structure:
- Black background with several axis-aligned colored line drawings.
- The line drawings are damaged by scattered pixels of one non-background noise color and by overlaps with other drawings.
- Each real drawing is a rectangular partitioned frame enclosing one or more background regions.

Rule:
- For each non-background color, reconstruct its horizontal and vertical wall segments from dense row/column evidence.
- Count the 4-connected background regions fully enclosed by those reconstructed walls.
- The color with no enclosed region is the noise/pad color.
- Sort drawing colors by enclosed-region count; equal counts are ordered by which reconstructed frame covers the other.

Key insight:
- The scattered color is not an object; it only pads the histogram, while each true object's room count is stable after rectilinear wall reconstruction.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="aaef0977" lines="25-41">
<description>
Replace an orange canvas with concentric Manhattan-distance color rings centered on the lone marker pixel.

Input structure:
- The grid is an orange background with exactly one non-orange marker pixel.
- The marker color is one of the colors in the fixed non-orange ring cycle.

Rule:
- The marker location is distance 0 and keeps the marker color.
- Each step of Manhattan distance away from the marker advances one position
  through the cycle green, yellow, black, grey, red, teal, maroon, pink, blue.
- Fill every pixel of the original grid by this distance-indexed cycle.

Key insight: The output is not copied from existing objects; it is the
distance field around the single marker, colored by a cyclic palette that
excludes the orange background.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="99caaf76" lines="56-75">
<description>
Move each marked cross object away from its marker to the grid edge while rotating the colored cross 180 degrees.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- Each 8-connected foreground object has nine pixels: a 4-pixel marker-color
  sprite attached to a 5-pixel non-marker colored cross.
- Each object bbox is either 3x6 or 6x3, so the marker sprite is on exactly
  one side of the cross.

Rule:
- Move the whole object straight away from its marker side until the object
  bbox touches the corresponding grid edge.
- Keep the marker sprite unchanged within the object.
- Rotate the 3x3 colored cross block by 180 degrees before placing it.

Key insight:
The marker sprite is a directional tail; the cross is the leading payload,
and only that payload turns around during the slide to the edge.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="7e2bad24" lines="91-106">
<description>
Extend the diagonal blue ray through the grid, reflecting it off red line-segment mirrors.

Input structure
- Black-background grid with one diagonal 8-connected blue ray seed.
- Red foreground objects, when present, are straight horizontal or vertical mirror segments.
- Other foreground bars are transparent to the ray and may be overwritten where the ray crosses them.

Rule
- Start from the ray seed endpoint whose outward diagonal extension remains inside the grid.
- Paint the ray continuously in that diagonal direction until it leaves the grid.
- If the next cell is a red mirror, do not paint it; reflect by flipping the row direction on a horizontal mirror or the column direction on a vertical mirror.

Key insight
- The non-red bars are decoys: only red bars reflect the diagonal path, while all other crossed cells become blue.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="11dc524f" lines="25-40">
<description>
Slide the non-square sprite to the square anchor and replace the anchor with the sprite's mirror image.

Input structure:
- The background is the most common color.
- There is one 8-connected non-background sprite that is not a solid 2x2 square.
- There is one solid 2x2 square anchor in another non-background color.

Rule:
- Move the sprite straight toward the anchor along their unique cardinal separation until it touches the anchor's near edge.
- Reflect the moved sprite across that near edge and draw the reflected copy in the anchor color.
- Clear the original locations; the output keeps only the moved sprite and reflected anchor-color copy.

Key insight:
- The 2x2 square marks the mirror line: its side facing the sprite becomes the contact edge for a two-color reflected pair.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="83b6b474" lines="95-111">
<description>
Translate the colored objects into the most balanced complete rectangular frame.

Input structure
A grid with one dominant background color and several monochromatic
4-connected foreground objects that are straight bars or corner-like line
sprites.

Rule
Preserve every foreground object exactly, allowing translation only, and
place the objects so they cover every pixel of a one-cell-thick rectangular
frame exactly once; the frame interior is the background color.

Key insight
The output shape is not copied from the input: the foreground pixel count
determines possible frame sizes, and the intended output is the valid frame
assembly with the least difference between height and width.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="eval" puzzle_id="2d0172a1" lines="209-224">
<description>
Convert a rough nested line drawing into a clean canonical frame-and-dot diagram.

Input structure:
- The grid has one background color and one foreground color.
- Foreground 4-connected objects are rough closed frames or small filled blobs.
- Frames may contain other frames/blobs; blobs outside a frame remain outside it.

Rule:
- Detect the containment tree of foreground objects using holes of each frame object.
- Replace every blob with one foreground pixel.
- Replace every frame with a rectangular border padded two cells around its direct children.
- Place siblings by their original row/column overlap and order, with one empty cell between canonical objects.

Key insight: The exact wobbly geometry is irrelevant; only enclosure plus relative sibling positions define the output.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="dce56571" lines="8-23">
<description>
Straighten the single foreground mask into a centered horizontal segment of the same color and length.

Input structure:
- The grid has one dominant background color.
- All non-background pixels share one foreground color.
- The number of foreground pixels fits in one centered row of the grid.

Rule:
- Erase the original foreground mask.
- Count its pixels, keep its color, and paint that many pixels as a horizontal segment.
- Place the segment on the middle row with equal background margin on the left and right.

Key insight:
- The original foreground shape is irrelevant except for its color and area; the output is the area normalized into the grid's central horizontal line.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="train" puzzle_id="46c35fc7" lines="21-34">
<description>
Rotate the colors around every 3x3 foreground ring, with corners and side-centers cycling in opposite directions.

Input structure
- The grid has a dominant background color.
- Each 4-connected foreground object is a complete 3x3 ring: eight foreground pixels around a background center.

Rule
- For each ring, move corner colors one step around the four corners.
- Move side-center colors one step around the four side centers in the opposite direction.
- Leave the background center and all other background pixels unchanged.

Key insight
- Corners and side centers form two separate four-cycles on the same 3x3 ring.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

